## One Tree

The Records in this folder were two disconnected forests: a tutorial of
chapters and ideas, and the project's own documents, sections and tasks. They
described the same subjects twice, under two different link names, with no
root. This is the work of making them one tree — a single parent link from a
single root, branching out into named ideas, each idea surrounded by its own
tasks.

**The shape is built.** One root, one parent link, `@reference` for anything
sideways, and every Record reachable from `First Steps` by walking down. The
documents are gone as a family: `Ontology` became `Record`, `Organ`,
`Assertion`, `Concept`, `Lingua`, `Query`, `Ledger Facts`, `Federation`,
`Protein`, `Sync`, `Visibility`, `Proximity` and `Trail`; `Karma` became
`Condition`, `Threshold`, `Consequence` and `Frequency`; the tutorial chapters
that taught the same nouns were folded into them, teaching on top and
specification below. What is left is the reading, not the wiring.


**A depth-first walk is the development order.** Siblings are ordered, so
reading the tree top to bottom gives Ontology, then Interface, then Transfer,
then Karma — and the work Records you pass on the way are the queue. That only
works because the tasks live beside their subject: the 113 open items that
were one ordered list under Ontology are now `Lingua`, `Visibility`,
`Federation`, `Karma`, `Trail`, `Sync`, `Interface` and
`How we work` — each branch carrying its own. A task Record sorts at 90,
after everything there is to read about the subject and before the next
subject begins.

**The tree is the reading order and the plan at once.** Walk it from the root
and you get the introduction, then the reason Lince exists, then each idea in
turn. Walk further down any branch and you reach the tasks that are left in it.
When a task lands, its text folds up into the prose of the idea it belonged to
and the task Record is deleted — so an idea's description thickens with what
was actually built, and what remains below it is always exactly what is left.

**The root is `First Steps` and it is the only Record without a parent** — the
canvas, the sands, and a first taste of what Lince is for. Under it, first, the
Death of Lince: why any of this exists at all. Then each subject as its own
branch, at whatever depth it needs. Being the root is a fact about links rather
than a label: nothing above it claims it, which is checkable, and a test
refuses a second one. Order is the number on the parent link, and it is a
decimal — inserting between 7 and 8 is `7.5`, which is how the Death of Lince
came to sit at `0.5`, ahead of a statute numbered from 1.

**State is the quantity and a Concept, and they are checked against each
other.** The number is what sorts, because "active work" is `quantity < -1` and
no set of unordered words can express a comparison. The Concept — `@stable`,
`@backlog`, `@todo`, `@wip` — is what a sand filters and colours. They are two
projections of one fact rather than two authorities: `check_lingua.js` refuses
a file whose word and number disagree, so the `-2 @wip` pairing the contract
always described is now something the folder can actually say.

**Names are ideas, not coordinates.** A Record is called `Organ`, `Sync`,
`Karma`, `Condition` — never `TODO - Karma - 1.2`, never `(document)`, never a
chapter number. A name is what you link to; a coordinate is what you have to
look up.

**This folder is layered, not flat, and the layers disagree.** Transfer grew a
large set of features, then almost all of Lince was rewritten, then Transfer
grew again — so two passages about one subject can both be careful, considered
writing and still contradict each other, with nothing on the page saying which
one is later. Age is read from the history, never from where a passage sits in
a file: `Transfer - OLD STUFF DOWN HERE` is at position 1 and is among the
oldest text here.

**Nothing is discarded by the merge.** Every passage lands in exactly one of
four places, and only the first two are the merge's decision to make. Text that
is **stable** becomes the idea's prose. Text that is **superseded** — the same
need met another way since — is deleted, and the entry that deletes it names
what replaced it. Text that is merely **different** — not superseded, not
contradicted, just a stray idea whose vibe does not match the body of work
around it, like an observer on a Transfer, or "if this one resolves, mine
resolves too" — is not blended in and not dropped: it becomes its own backlog
Record hanging off the subject it belongs to. Text that **contradicts** what is
built is deleted if it is small, and kept as backlog with the contradiction
stated plainly if it is large or important enough to be worth reopening.

**The point of the fourth pile is that vibe is information.** Two things at the
same state that feel nothing alike should not sit in one list, because the list
stops being readable and the odd one gets skimmed past. Backlog is cheap and
individual cards are easy to throw away later; a good idea quietly absorbed
into a paragraph it does not belong in is not recoverable. When in doubt, it
becomes a card.

- [ ] **Bring the nine First Steps ideas up to date.** The root now describes
      workspaces, the seeded Edit sand and the corner toolbar as they are, but
      its children still describe the frontend as it was: the toolbar contents,
      what edit mode turns on, and the sand catalog have all moved since. Read
      them against `app.rs`, `sand/shell.rs` and `domain/board.rs` rather
      than against each other.
- [ ] **Date the strata, subject by subject, and triage what disagrees.** The
      structure is one tree now; the PROSE inside it has not been read against
      itself. Transfer is the sharp case — its theory, its build order and its
      implemented state were written at three different times and `Building
      Transfer` now holds all three in one body without anything saying which
      is later. Same for `Sync`, which carries four generations of
      `What is built` / `Decided against` / `What is left`. Establish the
      order from git, then triage: stable into the prose, superseded deleted
      with its replacement named, different and contradicting promoted to
      their own backlog Records.
- [ ] **Read `Unsorted thoughts` and empty it.** A pasted conversation, kept
      at backlog so nothing in it is lost. Mine it into Protein and Interface
      Records, then delete it.
- [ ] **Give the remaining ontology nouns an opening.** `Record`, `Organ`,
      `Assertion` and `Concept` were met by a reader before being specified.
      `Lingua`, `Query`, `Ledger Facts`, `Federation`, `Protein`,
      `Sync`, `Visibility`, `Proximity` and `Trail` still open with their
      specification, which is the wrong altitude for someone arriving.
- [ ] **Promote the checkbox piles that deserve their own Records.** Several
      `— what is left` Records still hold a dozen unrelated items in one body.
      A task that needs breaking down should become a Record with a name.
- [ ] **Split `Later, still Rule` into Records.** One body carries fifteen
      numbered subjects — Command, Entries, Timeline, Authority, Effects,
      Signals, Senses, Learning, Attention, Automation Trust, Workflows,
      Imagination, Runtime operations — each of which is a subject somebody
      would want to link to and none of which is findable inside a single
      Record called "later". Same for `Building Transfer`, which is six
      former sections concatenated into one body.
- [ ] **Confirm the quantities I re-triaged.** Six Records moved from stable
      to backlog on my judgement rather than yours: `AniccaDB`, whose own
      title says proposal; `Nutrition` with `Optimizer` and `Sources`, a food
      optimizer that had been filed under Ontology; `Deterministic
      Simulation` with `How the simulation logic works`, which are notes on
      Turso's simulator; and `Unsorted thoughts`. Each reads as an idea
      rather than as something true today, but stable-to-backlog is a
      demotion and the call should be yours.
- [ ] **Move the reasoning to where the work went.** `The order of the work`
      still holds the *why* for the 113 items that moved out to their
      branches — thirteen groups of it, plus the BUILT notes. Each group
      belongs in the prose of the Record that now owns its tasks. Until then
      a reason and its task are one hop apart instead of together.
- [ ] **Check where I filed each group.** The 113 items were one flat list and
      their group boundaries were lost before this merge, so I recovered them
      from where the subject changes and filed each group under the Record
      that owns it now. The splits worth a second look: the public face and
      the three tiers both went to `Federation` rather than `Visibility`, and
      tree sync went to `Trail` rather than `Sync`.
